On a fanless 8-core M2 Air with 16 GB and a 256 GB SSD (the MC7X4 model is very likely the 8-core GPU), a 5 × 5 km free-roam world can realistically reach about parity with UE5.8 frames g–j within roughly 150 m of the camera, at a locked 30 fps once warm. That assumes about 1280x800 internal resolution with MetalFX temporal upscaling and a static or slow sun. The lighting would come from:
- PSSM/PCSS dappled sun;
- SDFGI at half resolution, or SSIL, for warm bounce;
- canopy sky-visibility and sun-transmittance maps baked from the real 0.5 m CHM (canopy height model), for the dark interior and for canopy shadow beyond the shadow-cascade range;
- froxel fog for haze and shafts;
- AgX tonemapping with auto-exposure.

This setup can beat UE5.8 in several areas, because none of them depend on the engine: measured 0.5 m terrain in place of stretched 1 m steps, real tree positions from the CHM, a physical sky, height-blended materials, understorey density, and far canopy tinted from ortho imagery. It will not beat UE5.8 on GI quality. Multi-bounce and off-screen bounce are missing, far canopy shadows will be softer than Virtual Shadow Maps would give, and mid-distance geometry will be less dense than Nanite. Changing time of day is expensive. My estimate is 80–90% of the valued lighting look; it rests on one real-data Godot render and has not been profiled on the Mac.

Going clearly beyond UE5.8 over a 5 km world would need two things. The first is better hardware: a fan-cooled Apple Silicon Mac from M3 onwards (M4 Pro or M5 class, with hardware ray tracing, MetalFX denoised upscaling and no throttling), or an RTX PC running Godot's Vulkan ray-tracing path once it matures. The second is better assets: species-accurate scanned Tsuga and Chamaecyparis foliage and bark, plus site capture for calibration. For non-interactive stills and fly-throughs, Blender Cycles on the same data already exceeds UE5.8's lighting today.
